### Which SOLID principles matter most in LLD?

In real low-level design, four principles do most of the heavy lifting: SRP, OCP, LSP, and DIP. ISP supports them by keeping interfaces small.

### How does SRP help in LLD?

Single Responsibility says a class should have one reason to change. A `TransferService` that also formats emails has two reasons to change; split the email part into `NotificationService`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// SRP applied: interest calculation is its own service
public class InterestService {
    public BigDecimal calculate(String accountType, BigDecimal balance) {
        if ("SAVINGS".equals(accountType)) {
            return balance.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.04"));
        }
        return balance.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.02"));
    }
}

// Notification is its own service
public class AlertService {
    public void sendInterestAlert(String customerId, BigDecimal interest) {
        System.out.println("Alert to " + customerId + ": Interest credited " + interest);
    }
}
```

A change to the SMS vendor never risks breaking interest math.

### How does OCP help in LLD?

Open/Closed says code is open for extension but closed for modification. Adding a new account product means adding a strategy class, not editing a growing if-else.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public interface InterestStrategy {
    BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal balance);
}

public class SavingsInterest implements InterestStrategy {
    public BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal balance) {
        return balance.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.04"));
    }
}

public class CurrentInterest implements InterestStrategy {
    public BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal balance) {
        return balance.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.02"));
    }
}

// Adding NRE: new class, no edits to existing strategies
public class NreInterest implements InterestStrategy {
    public BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal balance) {
        return balance.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.065"));
    }
}
```

The existing, regulator-approved logic is never re-tested and never re-risked.

### How does LSP help in LLD?

Liskov Substitution says a subclass must honour the parent's contract so it can replace the parent without surprises.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// LSP: any InterestStrategy drops in without special-case handling
public class InterestCalculator {
    private final InterestStrategy strategy;

    public InterestCalculator(InterestStrategy strategy) {
        this.strategy = strategy;
    }

    public BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal balance) {
        // Works for SavingsInterest, CurrentInterest, NreInterest – any
        return strategy.calculate(balance);
    }
}
```

If `Account.debit` never throws for valid input, a `SavingsAccount` must not suddenly throw for the same input.

### How does DIP help in LLD?

Dependency Inversion says high-level modules depend on abstractions, not concrete classes. Rajesh achieves this with constructor injection so he can swap implementations and mock in tests.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public interface CreditScoreProvider {
    int fetchScore(String customerId);
}

public class LoanService {
    private final CreditScoreProvider provider;

    public LoanService(CreditScoreProvider provider) {
        this.provider = provider;
    }

    public boolean approve(String customerId, BigDecimal amount) {
        return provider.fetchScore(customerId) > 700;
    }
}

// In tests: inject a mock
// LoanService svc = new LoanService(id -> 800);
// assertTrue(svc.approve("CUST-001", new BigDecimal("500000")));
```

### How does ISP support the other principles?

Interface Segregation keeps interfaces small so classes are not forced to implement unused methods. Small contracts are easier to honour fully (supporting LSP) and easier to mock (supporting DIP).

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// Small, focused interfaces
public interface InterestBearing {
    BigDecimal calculateInterest(BigDecimal balance);
}

public interface Overdraftable {
    BigDecimal overdraftLimit();
}

public interface Tradable {
    void tradeStock(String symbol, int qty);
}

// Savings implements only what it needs
public class SavingsAccount implements InterestBearing {
    public BigDecimal calculateInterest(BigDecimal balance) {
        return balance.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.04"));
    }
}

// Trading account implements trading + interest
public class TradingAccount implements InterestBearing, Tradable {
    public BigDecimal calculateInterest(BigDecimal balance) {
        return balance.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.02"));
    }
    public void tradeStock(String symbol, int qty) {
        System.out.println("Trade " + qty + " of " + symbol);
    }
}
```

### How do all five work together in a banking module?

Pankaj, the architect at National Bank of India, designed the interest module using all five:

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Map;

public class SolidInAction {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // OCP + DIP: strategies selected at runtime via map
        Map<String, InterestStrategy> strategies = Map.of(
            "SAVINGS", new SavingsInterest(),
            "CURRENT", new CurrentInterest(),
            "NRE", new NreInterest()
        );

        // LSP: any strategy drops in
        for (var entry : strategies.entrySet()) {
            BigDecimal interest = entry.getValue().calculate(new BigDecimal("1000000"));
            System.out.println(entry.getKey() + " interest on 10L: " + interest);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
SAVINGS interest on 10L: 40000.00
CURRENT interest on 10L: 20000.00
NRE interest on 10L: 65000.000
```

### When should you not apply SOLID mechanically?

SOLID is guidance, not religion. Applied mechanically it creates needless interfaces and one-line classes.

```java
// OVER-APPLIED: interface for a class that will never have a second implementation
public interface FlatFeeCalculator {
    BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal amount);
}
public class FlatFeeCalculatorImpl implements FlatFeeCalculator {
    public BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal amount) {
        return new BigDecimal("50.00");
    }
}

// BETTER: plain method until a second implementation actually appears
public class FeeService {
    public BigDecimal flatFee() {
        return new BigDecimal("50.00");
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- SOLID principles are tools to manage change and coupling where they genuinely exist.
- In Spring, DIP is automatic through constructor injection, and OCP is natural with bean registries.
- In a microservice, SRP maps to bounded contexts: each service has one business reason to exist.
- The real interview signal is knowing when to apply and when to hold back.
